<PlainLayout title="Running pilot behind a firewall — Pilot Protocol (plain)" description="Compat mode tunnels Pilot packets over HTTPS/WSS for daemons in UDP-blocked environments." canonical="https://pilotprotocol.network/plain/docs/firewalls/">

<p><a href="/plain/docs/">&larr; Docs index</a></p>

<h1>Running pilot behind a firewall</h1>

<p>When UDP is blocked, compatibility mode tunnels Pilot packets over HTTPS/WSS to the beacon. This uses the same overlay network with a different wire protocol.</p>

<h2>When to use compat mode</h2>
<p>The default Pilot daemon binds a public UDP socket. This does not work in certain environments.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Container platforms without UDP exposure, such as Render, Railway, Vercel, Fly.io's per-app DNS routing, or AWS Lambda. These platforms may not route inbound UDP or may use symmetric NAT.</li>
  <li>Corporate networks where outbound UDP is firewalled but HTTPS to internet hosts is allowed.</li>
  <li>Wireless networks that block all ports except TCP/443. As of v1.10.3, compat mode runs entirely on TCP/443.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the daemon registers but heartbeats fail or queries time out with relay-retransmit errors, it is likely in one of these environments. Compatibility mode provides an alternative wire protocol from the daemon to the beacon.</p>

<h2>Enabling compat mode</h2>
<p>Compat mode is enabled with a CLI flag on the standalone pilot-daemon binary.</p>
<pre><code>pilot-daemon -transport=compat</code></pre>
<p>As of v1.10.3, the daemon uses a single outbound TCP/443 connection. The explicit command form is:</p>
<pre><code>pilot-daemon \
  -transport=compat \
  -compat-beacon=wss://beacon.pilotprotocol.network/v1/compat \
  -tls-trust=system \
  -registry=registry.pilotprotocol.network:443 \
  -registry-tls -registry-trust=system</code></pre>
<p>Flag semantics:</p>
<ul>
  <li>-transport=compat — Switches the tunnel transport from UDP to WSS. The default is udp.</li>
  <li>-compat-beacon &lt;url&gt; — The beacon's WSS endpoint. The default is wss://beacon.pilotprotocol.network/v1/compat.</li>
  <li>-tls-trust &lt;mode&gt; — Defaults to system.</li>
  <li>-registry &lt;host:port&gt; — Defaults to registry.pilotprotocol.network:443 in compat mode.</li>
  <li>-registry-tls + -registry-trust=system — Auto-enabled in compat mode.</li>
</ul>
<p>The daemon also automatically forces relay_only=true when compat mode is enabled.</p>

<h2>How it works</h2>
<p>The compat-mode daemon opens outbound connections to TCP/443 only. Connections are multiplexed by SNI through a single nginx listener on the rendezvous host.</p>
<ul>
  <li>beacon.pilotprotocol.network:443 — A long-lived WebSocket Secure connection for the data plane.</li>
  <li>registry.pilotprotocol.network:443 — A TLS connection pool for the registry RPC channel.</li>
</ul>
<p>nginx routes traffic based on the TLS ClientHello's SNI field. After the TLS handshake, the daemon completes an Ed25519 challenge for authentication. Pilot UDP packets are sent as binary WebSocket frames.</p>
<p>The beacon bridges between UDP peers and WSS peers. To a specialist, a compat-mode daemon is identical to a symmetric-NAT peer. End-to-end Ed25519 trust is unchanged. TLS encrypts the channel between the daemon and beacon, while Ed25519 protects peer-to-peer identity and payload integrity.</p>

<h2>TLS trust and corporate proxies</h2>
<p>The public beacon presents a Let's Encrypt certificate. The default setting, -tls-trust=system, verifies this certificate against the operating system's trust store. This trust mode does not protect against TLS-intercepting proxies.</p>
<p>A future release will embed the Pilot Protocol root CA certificate and change the default to -tls-trust=pinned. This will verify the beacon's certificate against only this root. To use the daemon behind a TLS-intercepting corporate proxy, -tls-trust=system must be passed explicitly.</p>
<p>End-to-end Ed25519 protects peer identity and payload integrity in both trust modes. A TLS-intercepting proxy can read or censor relay traffic but cannot forge a specialist's signed reply.</p>

<h2>Limits</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Latency: Compat-mode traffic always traverses the beacon. One-way latency is expected to be ~50-200 ms to the nearest beacon region, plus the beacon-to-specialist hop.</li>
  <li>Bandwidth: Compat mode has higher overhead than UDP relay due to TCP/TLS framing.</li>
  <li>Deep Packet Inspection: Networks that block arbitrary outbound TLS will block compat mode.</li>
  <li>One beacon per session: The daemon holds one WSS connection at a time. It reconnects on disconnect.</li>
</ul>

